#Where a peptide bond may be attacked, and from which direction the attack
#counts. Read by peptidebond.sites.
#
#Format: <resname> <atomname> <antecedent> [[~|^]<plane>[:angle]]
#
#TWO LINES COVER EVERY AMINO ACID, because the peptide backbone is the same in
#all of them -- that is what makes it a backbone. '*' matches any residue, so an
#acyl group, a cap or a modified amino acid is covered too, instead of being
#silently left with no criterion because nobody thought to list it.

*  C  O  ^CA:75
*  N  CA

#THE ELECTROPHILE, '* C O ^CA:75'
#
#  C   the carbonyl carbon, which is what gets attacked.
#  O   its antecedent, so the base direction is OPPOSITE C=O.
#  ^CA the plane reference, '^' meaning the two directions LEAVE the (C, O, CA)
#      plane along its normal -- one on each face of the sp2 centre.
#  :75 tilted 75 degrees off the base direction, which puts them 105 degrees
#      from the C=O axis. That is where the carbonyl's pi* lobe is, and a
#      nucleophile arriving anywhere else does not reach it (Burgi & Dunitz,
#      1974). The ribosome's own pre-attack state, 1VY4, measures 100.6 degrees
#      between the A-site amine, the P-site carbonyl carbon and its oxygen, at
#      3.204 A.
#
#THE NUCLEOPHILE, '* N CA'
#
#  N   the attacking nitrogen.
#  CA  its only heavy neighbour, so its lone pair points away from CA, which is
#      the direction it attacks along.
#
#No plane for the nitrogen, and no '-C' either: a nitrogen that can attack is a
#free terminus by construction -- PeptideBondFormation::_isFree refuses one
#already bonded to another residue's carbonyl -- so CA is all it has. A
#mid-chain amide nitrogen does need the previous residue's C to point its
#hydrogen, but that is the hydrogen bond's business and lives in the .hbond.
#
#BOTH ENDS HAVE TO BE HERE. This file, when given, is the only table the
#peptide bond term reads; it does not fall back to the .hbond for a half it is
#missing. The angular factor is applied per end and SKIPPED when a site is
#absent, so a half-filled table makes a bond form MORE readily, not less.
#
#TO OVERRIDE ONE RESIDUE, name it: a line with a residue name beats '*' for that
#residue, and the rest keep the two lines above. Proline's nitrogen, for
#instance, carries CD as well as CA, which an N-terminal proline could use:
#
#    PRO  N  CA  CD
#
#ATOM NAMES ARE PLAIN PDB ONES and need no renamed twin. The peptide bond term
#already carries peptidebond.naming for its own 'bond = C:N' and resolves these
#through it, so one file serves a reduced topology and an unreduced one.
